-- A loop of tail calls through a handler whose clause captures a value bound outside the
-- handler, the twin of the kernel program `loop-captured` (Backend/Kernel.lean,
-- `Shapes.loopCaptured`): `go` calls itself in tail position, and each turn performs `yld`,
-- whose clause resumes in tail position but reads `c`, the handler's state, so each `yld`
-- suspends and is resumed by a state machine; the loop, the clause and the resume tail-call one
-- another, in constant stack. It is for the constant-stack check of `scripts/compile-native.sh`
-- (native, in a stack of 64 KiB).
--
-- 9000 turns, where the kernel's loop has 100000: a recursion is driven by `bounded`, whose
-- budget (`recBudget`, 10000) counts every call of the group, tail calls too, so 10^5 turns are
-- not expressible at the surface yet. A frame of at least 16 bytes per turn would still take
-- over 144 KiB, past the 64 KiB stack.
--
-- Answer: inl (); `live 0`. The kernel's answer is (): `exhausted_to_option` puts it in `inl`,
-- and a spent budget would answer `inr ()`. A resume that grew the stack per turn would
-- overflow; a suspension that was not freed would leave objects live.

include rec

effect Y { must_resume ctl yld() }

handler pass[A](c: i32): Y, A -> A = { return(a) = a; yld(k) = { let _ = c; k(()) } }

fn go(n: i32) -> 1 = if n == 0 { () } else { yld(); go(n - 1) }

main -> 1 + 1 =
  let c = 7;
  with exhausted_to_option[1] { with pass[1](c) { go(9000) } }
